     1	# BT8858A/AIC8800 BLE Porting Layout
     2	
     3	This directory keeps two different Bluetooth paths separated:
     4	
     5	- `bt_controller/hci_h4/`: current openVela/Zblue controller transport. It powers and configures the AIC controller, owns UART2 H4 traffic, and registers `/dev/ttyHCI0`.
     6	- `bt_controller/vela_compat/`: small compatibility shims required by the openVela Bluetooth service when only BLE is enabled.
     7	- `bt_stack/vela_ble/`: current Vela BLE host integration helpers. These files wrap Vela/Zblue APIs for local commands; they do not replace the Vela Bluetooth host stack.
     8	  - `common/`: shared helper code used by the local BLE command tools.
     9	  - `gap/`: GAP advertising helpers.
    10	  - `gatt/`: GATT server lifecycle helpers.
    11	- `bt_osal/`: legacy OS adaptation layer. Only the NuttX implementation is kept in the Vela tree.
    12	- `bt_stack/legacy_goc/`: old vendor/GOC command stack code. It is not the current Vela BLE data path, but is kept for `btstart --legacy` / `btstop --legacy` and reference.
    13	  - `include/`: legacy public API/types/config headers copied from the old stack.
    14	  - `profiles/hid/`: legacy HID wrapper kept from the old GOC command stack.
    15	
    16	Current BLE testing should use the Vela HCI path plus GAP/GATT helpers. The legacy GOC path should not be extended for new BLE features unless a legacy compatibility issue must be diagnosed.
    17	
    18	Runtime lifecycle:
    19	
    20	- Start the controller once with `btstart`, then start `bluetoothd &` once.
    21	- Application-level start/stop should control GAP/GATT activity, such as advertising, connections, notifications, and scan sessions.
    22	- Do not repeatedly kill and restart `bluetoothd` for normal BLE business logic. The current Vela bluetoothd lifecycle is not treated as a stable app-level toggle.
    23	- `btstop --force-daemon` is reserved for diagnostics or reboot preparation. Default `btstop` refuses to stop HCI while `bluetoothd` is still alive, so it does not leave a half-stopped service behind.
    24	
    25	## `nsh_lvgl/defconfig` Notes
    26	
    27	The D13X Vela board uses AIC8800D40L, so the verified Bluetooth target is BLE
    28	GAP/GATT. Some framework options still look broader than BLE because the current
    29	openVela Bluetooth service and Zblue SAL are not completely split into pure-BLE
    30	and BR/EDR-only build units.
    31	
    32	- `CONFIG_AIC_TEST_ADC`, `CONFIG_DRIVERS_WIRELESS`, and `CONFIG_PIPES` are not
    33	  BLE-specific requirements. `DRIVERS_WIRELESS` is selected by
    34	  `AIC_BT_BT8858A_VELA_HCI`, and `PIPES` is selected by `NET_LOCAL`, so
    35	  `savedefconfig` may omit their explicit lines while the generated `.config`
    36	  still enables them. `AIC_TEST_ADC` is different: the current
    37	  `app/Kconfig`/`app/Make.defs` do not source or build `app/testadc`, so adding
    38	  `CONFIG_AIC_TEST_ADC=y` to this defconfig is not an effective BT change and
    39	  will not make the test command part of the image unless the testadc app is
    40	  wired into the board application Kconfig/Make.defs separately.
    41	
    42	- `CONFIG_KVDB`, `CONFIG_KVDB_DIRECT`, and `CONFIG_UNQLITE` provide Bluetooth
    43	  service storage support. The openVela Bluetooth service has a storage-method
    44	  choice based on KVDB or UnQLite, and it stores adapter/device properties and
    45	  bond-related state through that layer. `KVDB_DIRECT` keeps access local and
    46	  avoids requiring a separate KVDB server process for this board. `UNQLITE` is
    47	  the persistent database backend used by the KVDB path.
    48	
    49	- `CONFIG_LIBUV_EXTENSION` is required by `CONFIG_BLUETOOTH`; the Bluetooth
    50	  framework depends on both `LIBUV` and `LIBUV_EXTENSION`. The extension also
    51	  provides Vela utility wrappers used by framework/service code.
    52	
    53	- `CONFIG_MBEDTLS_THREADING_C` and `CONFIG_MBEDTLS_THREADING_PTHREAD` enable the
    54	  Mbed TLS threading abstraction and back it with pthread mutexes. Bluetooth
    55	  service, Zblue, libuv, and KVDB/UnQLite run in multiple tasks/threads, so the
    56	  Mbed TLS global crypto state should use real locks. This is independent from
    57	  `CONFIG_MBEDTLS_NET_C`; the BLE port does not explicitly change the Mbed TLS
    58	  net socket helper setting.
    59	
    60	- `CONFIG_NET_LOCAL` enables Unix domain sockets. It is needed when the Bluetooth
    61	  framework uses socket IPC: `bluetoothd` and clients create `PF_LOCAL/AF_LOCAL`
    62	  sockets under the local VFS path. `NET_LOCAL` also selects `PIPES`.
    63	
    64	- `CONFIG_PTHREAD_MUTEX_TYPES` enables recursive/errorcheck pthread mutex types
    65	  and the corresponding `pthread_mutexattr_settype()` APIs. UnQLite's threaded
    66	  build and some Vela/Zblue profile code expect recursive mutex support, so this
    67	  option prevents build or runtime gaps in those dependencies.
    68	
    69	- `CONFIG_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_STACK_SIZE=8192` raises the Zblue system workqueue
    70	  stack from the small default. Zblue schedules connection, GATT, RPA, and other
    71	  host callbacks through workqueues; 8192 keeps enough margin for the current
    72	  BLE service path and avoids stack-sensitive crashes during GATT activity.
    73	
    74	- `CONFIG_MBEDTLS_NET_C` and `CONFIG_MEDIA` are not kept as BLE-specific changes.
    75	  `CONFIG_MBEDTLS_NET_C` controls Mbed TLS TCP/UDP socket helpers, which are not
    76	  required by GAP/GATT. `CONFIG_MEDIA` is only needed by media profiles such as
    77	  A2DP, and AIC8800D40L is currently treated as BLE-only.
